# Line endings are LF IN THE REPOSITORY, always.
#
# This is not a style preference. This repo is developed on Windows, where git's
# core.autocrlf checks files out as CRLF -- and CRLF found its way into the committed
# blobs. A shell script stored with CRLF fails on Linux at its first line:
#
#     bash: setup/install_llamacpp.sh: line 2: $'\r': command not found
#
# ...which would have broken the primary onboarding path for everyone who clones, while
# working perfectly on the machine it was written on. The setup scripts are the first
# thing a new user runs.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises on commit and checks out LF everywhere, so what is stored
# is what Linux runs. The explicit lines below are belt-and-braces for the files where a
# stray CR is fatal rather than cosmetic.

* text=auto eol=lf

*.sh   text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.ptx  text eol=lf
LICENSE text eol=lf
